      <abstract>
        <para>
          When the Linux kernel panics, &kdump; saves the memory of the crashed kernel to a
          file, the vmcore, so that you can find out what caused the crash. &kdump; is built on
          &kexec;: it keeps a second kernel ready in reserved memory and starts it the moment
          the main kernel crashes. This article explains how to set up &kdump; on
          &productname;. You install and enable &kdump;, reserve enough memory for it and test
          that it captures dumps. Finally, you analyze a captured dump.
        </para>
        <para><emphasis role="bold">Prerequisites</emphasis></para>
        <itemizedlist>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              Enough free disk space at the dump target. By default, &kdump; filters and
              compresses the dump, so it is smaller than the system memory.
            </para>
          </listitem>
        </itemizedlist>
      </abstract>
